WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.275
Lesson 5.1.

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:04.430
Bridges and trust assumptions.

00:00:04.430 --> 00:00:06.544
By the end, you'll be able to choose

00:00:06.544 --> 00:00:08.944
a bridge route by its actual trust model,

00:00:08.944 --> 00:00:11.515
and size your own exposure to it accordingly.

00:00:12.815 --> 00:00:14.105
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.105 --> 00:00:16.711
The three genuinely different ways a bridge

00:00:16.711 --> 00:00:19.320
can actually move value between chains.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.458
What a trust model actually is, and

00:00:21.458 --> 00:00:24.075
exactly who gets to approve a transfer.

00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:26.008
Why bridges specifically end up

00:00:26.008 --> 00:00:28.120
as such frequent hack targets.

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:30.654
And a full worked example, routing a real

00:00:30.654 --> 00:00:33.430
amount of money across a real bridge decision.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:37.005
Three genuinely different ways to bridge.

00:00:38.305 --> 00:00:39.739
Here are all three ways

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:42.190
value actually moves between chains.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.413
A canonical bridge is a chain's own official route,

00:00:45.413 --> 00:00:49.255
like a rollup's native bridge, usually carrying the strongest

00:00:49.255 --> 00:00:53.150
security available, sometimes at the cost of being slower.

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:56.316
Lock-and-mint locks your asset on the source chain,

00:00:56.316 --> 00:00:58.995
and mints a wrapped copy on the destination

00:00:58.995 --> 00:01:01.369
chain, a copy that's only ever as good

00:01:01.369 --> 00:01:04.165
as whatever's actually backing that lock.

00:01:04.165 --> 00:01:07.381
And a liquidity network simply pays you out directly

00:01:07.381 --> 00:01:10.718
on the other side, fast, but specifically dependent on

00:01:10.718 --> 00:01:14.420
that network's own contracts and its own available liquidity.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.959
Here's the actual trade-off each of these three makes,

00:01:18.959 --> 00:01:21.491
side by side, since none of them is simply

00:01:21.491 --> 00:01:24.265
better than the others in every situation.

00:01:24.265 --> 00:01:27.455
Canonical: strongest security, sometimes slower.

00:01:27.455 --> 00:01:30.874
Lock-and-mint: fast, but the wrapped copy's real value

00:01:30.874 --> 00:01:34.095
depends entirely on the lock behind it holding.

00:01:34.095 --> 00:01:37.272
Liquidity network: fastest of all three, but you're now

00:01:37.272 --> 00:01:40.675
trusting that network's own contracts and its own liquidity

00:01:40.675 --> 00:01:43.114
being genuinely available when you need it.

00:01:44.414 --> 00:01:46.807
Here's a practical way to match these three

00:01:46.807 --> 00:01:49.201
types to what you're actually trying to do,

00:01:49.201 --> 00:01:52.329
rather than defaulting to whichever one loads first.

00:01:52.329 --> 00:01:55.673
Moving a genuinely large amount, with no particular

00:01:55.673 --> 00:01:59.509
urgency, is exactly the canonical route's own strength.

00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:02.649
Moving a small amount, where speed actually matters

00:02:02.649 --> 00:02:05.185
more than squeezing out the very last bit

00:02:05.185 --> 00:02:08.022
of security, is exactly where a fast liquidity

00:02:08.022 --> 00:02:09.894
network earns its keep instead.

00:02:11.194 --> 00:02:12.284
Quick check.

00:02:12.284 --> 00:02:14.799
What actually backs a lock-and-mint wrapped token?

00:02:19.219 --> 00:02:22.272
The answer: the assets locked on the source chain,

00:02:22.272 --> 00:02:25.684
and the bridge's own security actually holding that lock.

00:02:26.984 --> 00:02:28.699
What a trust model actually is.

00:02:29.999 --> 00:02:33.065
Here's exactly what decides a bridge's real trust

00:02:33.065 --> 00:02:36.070
model, the single most important question to ask

00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:38.394
about any bridge before using it.

00:02:38.394 --> 00:02:41.762
Who's actually allowed to approve a transfer moving

00:02:41.762 --> 00:02:44.223
through it: a multisig held by just a

00:02:44.223 --> 00:02:47.072
handful of keys, a broader validator set, a

00:02:47.072 --> 00:02:50.505
light client verifying the source chain directly, or

00:02:50.505 --> 00:02:52.942
genuine fraud or validity proofs.

00:02:52.942 --> 00:02:55.524
Fewer, weaker signers means more risk,

00:02:55.524 --> 00:02:58.437
in a direct, almost mechanical relationship.

00:02:59.737 --> 00:03:02.423
Worth being precise about why this specific

00:03:02.423 --> 00:03:05.352
question matters more than almost anything else

00:03:05.352 --> 00:03:07.794
about a bridge, including its own brand

00:03:07.794 --> 00:03:10.352
name or how long it's been operating.

00:03:10.352 --> 00:03:12.962
A bridge secured by a small multisig can

00:03:12.962 --> 00:03:15.700
be drained the moment enough of those keys

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:19.011
are compromised, regardless of how much total value

00:03:19.011 --> 00:03:22.322
it's ever safely processed before that exact moment.

00:03:23.622 --> 00:03:26.604
Here's how to actually check a bridge's real trust

00:03:26.604 --> 00:03:30.647
model yourself, before ever routing meaningful value through it.

00:03:30.647 --> 00:03:34.250
Find its own documentation, specifically for how transfers

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:38.092
actually get approved, not just its marketing description.

00:03:38.092 --> 00:03:41.974
Count how many signers, or validators, are genuinely required,

00:03:41.974 --> 00:03:45.602
and how independent they actually are from each other.

00:03:45.602 --> 00:03:48.330
And check whether it's ever published a real

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:52.392
security audit covering that exact approval mechanism specifically.

00:03:53.692 --> 00:03:56.121
Worth making the multisig risk concrete,

00:03:56.121 --> 00:03:58.317
rather than leaving it abstract.

00:03:58.317 --> 00:04:01.623
If a bridge relies on a five-of-eight multisig, an

00:04:01.623 --> 00:04:05.382
attacker only needs to compromise five specific keys, out

00:04:05.382 --> 00:04:08.729
of eight, to move everything that bridge holds.

00:04:08.729 --> 00:04:12.585
That's a genuinely finite, achievable target, especially

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.426
against keys held by individuals or small

00:04:15.426 --> 00:04:18.809
teams, not some enormous, near-impossible barrier.

00:04:20.109 --> 00:04:21.199
Quick check.

00:04:21.199 --> 00:04:24.325
What's the actual relationship between the number of

00:04:24.325 --> 00:04:26.744
signers a bridge relies on, and its risk?

00:04:31.164 --> 00:04:35.187
The answer: fewer, weaker signers means more risk, directly,

00:04:35.187 --> 00:04:38.814
since compromising a smaller group is genuinely easier.

00:04:40.114 --> 00:04:42.359
Why bridges are such frequent targets.

00:04:43.659 --> 00:04:46.925
Worth being precise about the actual mechanism that

00:04:46.925 --> 00:04:50.316
makes bridges such attractive targets, since it isn't

00:04:50.316 --> 00:04:52.689
random or purely about weak code.

00:04:52.689 --> 00:04:57.023
Bridges hold large, concentrated pools of value, specifically because

00:04:57.023 --> 00:05:00.614
every single user crossing that bridge needs their assets

00:05:00.614 --> 00:05:03.762
to sit somewhere while the transfer completes.

00:05:03.762 --> 00:05:08.777
That concentration, combined with genuinely complex cross-chain logic, is

00:05:08.777 --> 00:05:12.436
exactly the combination an attacker is looking for: a

00:05:12.436 --> 00:05:15.757
lot to gain, in one place, through one mechanism.

00:05:17.058 --> 00:05:19.562
Worth also naming why this pattern has

00:05:19.562 --> 00:05:22.323
repeated itself so often across DeFi's own

00:05:22.323 --> 00:05:25.833
history, rather than being an occasional accident.

00:05:25.833 --> 00:05:29.276
Complex cross-chain logic is genuinely hard to get

00:05:29.276 --> 00:05:32.450
perfectly right, and a single flaw anywhere in

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:35.218
that logic can expose the entire pool at

00:05:35.218 --> 00:05:38.421
once, not just one user's individual funds.

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:41.085
That's structurally different from a bug in

00:05:41.085 --> 00:05:44.355
an ordinary single-chain contract, which usually only

00:05:44.355 --> 00:05:46.111
exposes its own smaller pool.

00:05:47.411 --> 00:05:48.501
Quick check.

00:05:48.501 --> 00:05:51.746
Why are bridges such frequent hack targets, specifically?

00:05:56.166 --> 00:06:00.163
The answer: they concentrate large amounts of value behind

00:06:00.163 --> 00:06:03.889
genuinely complex, cross-chain logic, all in one place.

00:06:05.188 --> 00:06:06.193
Worked example.

00:06:07.493 --> 00:06:10.202
Here's exactly how to actually route this,

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:13.162
step by step, applying everything from earlier

00:06:13.162 --> 00:06:15.478
in this lesson to a real amount.

00:06:15.478 --> 00:06:17.688
Use the canonical route for the bulk

00:06:17.688 --> 00:06:20.018
of the fifty thousand dollars, even if

00:06:20.018 --> 00:06:22.946
it's genuinely slower than the alternatives.

00:06:22.946 --> 00:06:26.631
Use a reputable, well-established fast bridge only for a

00:06:26.631 --> 00:06:31.521
small, specifically time-sensitive portion, if you actually need speed.

00:06:31.521 --> 00:06:34.229
And send a small test transaction first, on any

00:06:34.229 --> 00:06:36.881
route, before committing the full amount to it.

00:06:38.181 --> 00:06:40.852
Here's the actual reasoning behind splitting it

00:06:40.852 --> 00:06:43.468
this way, rather than simply picking whichever

00:06:43.468 --> 00:06:45.716
single route seems most convenient.

00:06:45.716 --> 00:06:48.719
The bulk of the value genuinely benefits from the

00:06:48.719 --> 00:06:52.563
canonical route's stronger security, since size is exactly what

00:06:52.563 --> 00:06:55.806
makes a position worth protecting most carefully.

00:06:55.806 --> 00:06:58.633
A small, urgent portion can reasonably accept

00:06:58.633 --> 00:07:02.075
a faster bridge's slightly different risk, specifically

00:07:02.075 --> 00:07:04.656
because the amount at stake, if something

00:07:04.656 --> 00:07:07.176
did go wrong, is deliberately kept small.

00:07:08.476 --> 00:07:11.113
Here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:07:11.113 --> 00:07:13.810
example exists to prove, worth holding onto

00:07:13.810 --> 00:07:16.931
more than the specific dollar figure involved.

00:07:16.931 --> 00:07:19.898
Never leave a large balance sitting as a wrapped

00:07:19.898 --> 00:07:22.683
asset from a weak bridge, for any longer than

00:07:22.683 --> 00:07:25.893
it genuinely has to, since that wrapped token's real

00:07:25.893 --> 00:07:28.315
value is only ever as good as the lock,

00:07:28.315 --> 00:07:31.221
or the trust model, actually standing behind it.

00:07:32.521 --> 00:07:33.611
Quick check.

00:07:33.611 --> 00:07:36.786
For fifty thousand dollars, fast bridge, or canonical?

00:07:41.206 --> 00:07:44.068
The answer: canonical for the bulk of it; a

00:07:44.068 --> 00:07:47.581
fast bridge only for a small, genuinely urgent amount.

00:07:48.881 --> 00:07:51.902
Here's why sending a small test transaction first

00:07:51.902 --> 00:07:54.378
is worth the extra few minutes it costs,

00:07:54.378 --> 00:07:57.276
on any bridge, even one you've used before.

00:07:57.276 --> 00:08:00.706
It catches a wrong destination address, an unexpected

00:08:00.706 --> 00:08:03.373
fee, or a genuinely broken route, all for

00:08:03.373 --> 00:08:05.850
the cost of a small amount, instead of

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:09.026
discovering the exact same problem with your full

00:08:09.026 --> 00:08:11.566
balance already committed and in flight.

00:08:12.866 --> 00:08:14.326
Here's your checklist.

00:08:14.326 --> 00:08:17.121
Do it before bridging anything of real size.

00:08:17.121 --> 00:08:19.824
Write down the actual trust model of every

00:08:19.824 --> 00:08:23.016
bridge you use, explicitly, not just its name.

00:08:23.016 --> 00:08:25.467
Prefer the canonical route specifically for

00:08:25.467 --> 00:08:27.326
size, even when it's slower.

00:08:27.326 --> 00:08:31.015
And cap your own wrapped-asset exposure, deliberately, the same

00:08:31.015 --> 00:08:33.781
way you'd cap any other single point of failure.

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:36.326
Let's recap.

00:08:36.326 --> 00:08:39.945
Three bridge types: canonical, lock-and-mint, and liquidity

00:08:39.945 --> 00:08:43.441
network, each making a genuinely different trade-off.

00:08:43.441 --> 00:08:46.509
A bridge's trust model, who can actually approve

00:08:46.509 --> 00:08:49.703
a transfer, is the single most important question,

00:08:49.703 --> 00:08:53.071
and fewer, weaker signers always means more risk.

00:08:53.071 --> 00:08:56.764
Bridges are frequent targets specifically because they

00:08:56.764 --> 00:09:00.086
concentrate large value behind complex logic.

00:09:00.086 --> 00:09:03.165
And for real size, use the canonical route, keep

00:09:03.165 --> 00:09:06.684
fast bridges for small, urgent amounts, and never leave

00:09:06.684 --> 00:09:09.386
a large balance wrapped from a weak bridge.

00:09:10.686 --> 00:09:13.945
Worth connecting this directly back to the dependency

00:09:13.945 --> 00:09:16.841
map from this module's own Mastery Starter.

00:09:16.841 --> 00:09:19.384
A bridge is exactly one specific link in that

00:09:19.384 --> 00:09:22.093
chain, and now you know exactly what to actually

00:09:22.093 --> 00:09:24.360
check on it: which of the three types it

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.234
is, who's allowed to approve a transfer through it,

00:09:27.234 --> 00:09:29.888
and how much of your own balance you're willing

00:09:29.888 --> 00:09:32.265
to leave sitting on the wrapped side of it

00:09:32.265 --> 00:09:33.371
at any given moment.

00:09:34.671 --> 00:09:38.047
Write down every bridge's trust model, prefer canonical

00:09:38.047 --> 00:09:41.121
for size, and cap your wrapped-asset exposure.

00:09:41.121 --> 00:09:43.352
Next up, Lesson 5.2: layer

00:09:43.352 --> 00:09:46.162
2s, sequencers & withdrawal paths.
